    <h2>When a rewire makes sense</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Knob-and-tube wiring</strong>, common in homes built before the 1940s, which has no ground and was never meant to be covered by insulation.</li>
        <li><strong>Aluminum branch wiring</strong> from the late 1960s and 70s, whose connections loosen over time.</li>
        <li><strong>Two-prong outlets throughout</strong>, which usually mean no ground anywhere.</li>
        <li><strong>A remodel</strong>, when the walls are open anyway and rewiring costs a fraction of what it would later.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>How we plan it</h2>
    <p>We map every circuit first, then plan new home runs so kitchens, bathrooms and laundry get their own dedicated circuits. You get a fixed price, a schedule room by room, and a clear note of anything we will need to open.</p>
